<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ifá</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fá</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ifá is a system of divination that originated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, within the traditional religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a>. According to Ifá teaching it is overseen by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> Orunmila, who is believed to have given it to humanity. Its initiates, called babaláwo if male and ìyánífá if female, consult a body of 256 signs, the Odù, using a divining chain or palm nuts on a wooden divination tray, the ọpọ́n Ifá. Yoruba influence spread Ifá to neighbouring peoples such as the Fon, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> carried it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where it survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ifa.jpg" alt="Ifá" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Toluaye · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jogo_de_Ikin_Orossi.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
